PSEUDOTERMINAL / VIRTUAL SCREEN - 80x24

DEVICE INTERFACES
  /dev/ptmx          master, exclusive O_RDWR
  /dev/pts/0         slave (available while master is open)
  /dev/pts/0.screen  80x24 ANSI-rendered screen
  /dev/pts/0.key     named-key write for UP/DOWN/LEFT/RIGHT,
                     ENTER, ESC, TAB, SPACE, BACKSPACE,
                     DELETE, CTRL_A ... CTRL_Z

A program may redirect FDs 0..2 to the slave and spawn a child.
Master read/write transfers raw PTY bytes. The kernel also renders
common ANSI cursor/erase/reverse sequences on .screen; CSI 6n
reports cursor position. This is NOT a full terminal emulator.
No scrollback, resize, or atomic screen snapshot is provided.

MCP PERSISTENT PTY
  mcpd opens one PTY and starts interactive sh at startup.
  It uses screen-only mode; raw output ring is not accumulated.
  terminal_read({}) reads current ANSI screen once. A screen read
  during drawing may show an intermediate frame.
  terminal_send({text:'ls'}) accepts literal characters only.
  terminal_send({key:'ENTER'}) sends Enter; named keys are distinct
  from text. Accepted input is not evidence of completed rendering.
  Shell and foreground app share this PTY across all MCP clients.
  See /man/terminal-operations for state-aware action sequence.

SCREEN MARKERS
  ESC[7m/ESC[0m marks inverse-video cells.
  ESC[4m/ESC[24m underlines the visible cursor cell, including
  blank cells. The cursor can be hidden with CSI ?25l and shown
  again with CSI ?25h. When hidden, no cursor marker is emitted.
  This cursor marks the terminal location, not a vi document
  position or a Reversi legal move. ANSI sequences may be split
  across reads in raw device access.

For a physical terminal, pty_test can relay commands such as:
  pty_test fd
  pty_test sh
  pty_test vi /tmp/test.txt
Ctrl+C interrupts the foreground task. mcpd and the launching shell
have limited kill protection against MCP descendants only; this is
not an authentication boundary.
